(b) Whereas Article 12 of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights and Article 17 of the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights state that no person's honour and reputation shall be attacked, and that every person has the right to obtain legal protection against such attacks, to pave the way for that legal protection to be afforded;

(c) Whereas Article 19 of the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights states that the right to freedom of expression may be restricted by law in order to protect the reputation of others, to determine, under Article 16(a) of the Constitution, the circumstances in which that right may be restricted;

(d) Whereas Article 27 of the Constitution states that freedom of expression is a right afforded to every person, and whereas Article 67 of the Constitution states that the rights and freedoms afforded to every person and the responsibilities and duties that person must fulfil are two inseparable principles, to bring about a balance between the free expression of views in society and the protection, safeguarding and preservation of the reputation of persons in society

