(e) To determine that defamation is a civil wrong;

(f) To determine in law what constitutes defamation, the circumstances that shall not be deemed defamation, and the circumstances in which privilege is afforded;

(g) To pave the way for resolving defamation matters, before the stage of court proceedings or in place of the stage of court proceedings, by using other means permitted by law and empowered by law;

(h) To establish a system for filing an action seeking compensation for the damage suffered by a defamed party; and

(i) To determine the principles to be followed in awarding compensation for the damage suffered by a defamed party.

Principles of the Act
3. This Act is founded upon upholding and preserving the following principles. Every Article of this Act shall be read, and acted upon, in a manner that does not derogate from any of those principles.

(a) Recognising that having the name and reputation of a person protected is a

[stamp] [signature]

fundamental right afforded to every person by the Constitution, the international conventions to which the Maldives is a party, and this Act;

(b) Recognising that having the name and reputation of a person protected is a right afforded to natural persons and legal persons; and

(c) Recognising that, unless otherwise specifically provided in another Act, the right to freedom of expression and the right to freedom of the press shall not be restricted except to the extent set out in this Act for the protection of the name and reputation of a person.

Scope of Application of the Act
4. (a) This Act shall apply to the following parties.

(1) All natural persons;

(2) All legal persons;

(3) All media registered and operating in the Maldives, including print, broadcast and digital media; and

(4) All digital platforms and intermediaries, including social media platforms, content hosting services and other online service providers.

