(a) The standard that may be taken into consideration in deeming or not deeming an act to be an act of defamation, based on the nature of the various acts of defamation and the various types of means used to commit such an act;

(b) The matters that may be taken into consideration in determining whether an act of defamation was committed knowingly and intentionally, or unknowingly and in good faith;

(c) The matters that may be submitted by the claimant in establishing a defamation action;

(d) The permissible defences that may be submitted by the claimant in defending against a defamation action;

(e) The standard for identifying the party who committed an act of defamation, having regard to the nature of the act and the means used to commit it, in circumstances where it is not clearly known which particular party committed the act of defamation.

Chapter Four
Circumstances in which Privilege is Afforded

Granting of Absolute Privilege
18. (a) Articles 19 and 20 of this Act set out the circumstances in which it is determined that parties who make a defamatory statement or give such information shall not be required to bear responsibility, and in which, upon a defamation action being brought, those parties are afforded absolute

[stamp] [signature]

intention, that the statement made or the information given is a statement or information based on true facts;

(b) The making of that statement or the giving of that information not being prohibited under any law;

(c) There being a legal, professional, social, moral, public or other interest or duty permitted by law to make that statement or provide that information;

(d) The statement not having been made, or the information not having been given, with ill intent or to achieve an improper purpose; and

(e) The extent to which the statement was made or the information was published and disseminated being an extent that is necessarily required, to a reasonable degree, having regard to the circumstances.

Privilege Afforded in Relation to Investigation Reports
23. Where the conditions specified in Article 22 of this Act are fulfilled, the stating or publishing of something defamatory of a particular party in a report disclosed, after investigating a matter, by a party appointed to conduct an investigation under the authority of a law, shall not be deemed an act of defamation for the purposes of this Act.

Privilege Afforded in Relation to Matters Involving the Public Interest
24. Having regard to the privilege of matters involving the public interest, parties who do any of the following, where the conditions specified in Article 22 of this Act are fulfilled, shall be afforded privilege subject to conditions

[stamp] [signature]

privilege. Privilege subject to conditions shall be afforded under this Article only if it is believed with honest intention that the matter is one based on true facts.

(a) Disclosing the proceedings of the People's Majlis or of a committee of that Majlis, for the purpose of disclosing those proceedings, without adding to or omitting from what took place;

(b) Disclosing expressions contained in a document published by the People's Majlis as public information, or part of such document, as it stands, without addition or omission;

(c) Disclosing the proceedings of a court hearing, for the purpose of disclosing those proceedings, without adding to or omitting from what took place;

(d) Disclosing expressions contained in a document published by a court, or by a judge or employee of a court, or published with the permission of any such party, or part thereof, as it stands, without addition or omission;

(e) Disclosing, as it stands and without addition or omission, a report issued by an institution vested with the legal power to investigate, after investigating a matter under the authority of a law, or part of such report;

(f) By any office of the State, in accordance with law,

